WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.715 --> 00:00:10.340
JEFF JOHNSON: Welcome
back, developers.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.790
Jeff here, and I'm joined
once again by our friend Alpha

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:15.100
from Pivotal.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.520
Alpha is here to
tell us about how

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.980
to handle monitoring and
logging to our Cloud Foundry

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.300
applications.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.920
Last time we were
together, we talked

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.884
about how to monitor
our infrastructure,

00:00:23.884 --> 00:00:25.550
but now we're talking
about the Foundry.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:26.420
What's next, Alpha?

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.010
ALPHA CHEN: The next step is
connecting the Cloud Foundry

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.800
logging and metrics pipeline,
also called Loggregator,

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:31.580
to Stackdriver.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.038
JEFF JOHNSON: So
this would be data

00:00:33.038 --> 00:00:35.690
ranging from running application
logs to health metrics

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.259
such as container CPU usage.

00:00:37.259 --> 00:00:38.550
ALPHA CHEN: That's right, Jeff.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.980
Loggregator is
designed to hook up

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:43.332
to external data sinks, which
Cloud Foundry calls nozzles.

00:00:43.332 --> 00:00:45.290
The Stackdriver nozzle
connects to the pipeline

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.925
through UDP today, potentially
switching to TCP in the future,

00:00:48.925 --> 00:00:50.300
and receives a
[INAUDIBLE] stream

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.825
of data from Cloud Foundry to
prevent backups in the system.

00:00:52.825 --> 00:00:54.366
The nozzle can be
scaled horizontally

00:00:54.366 --> 00:00:57.104
as necessary to handle
increased log traffic.

00:00:57.104 --> 00:00:59.270
JEFF JOHNSON: That's why
we, and I mean a great team

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.620
from Pivotal and
Google, including us,

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.380
developed the
Stackdriver nozzle.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.762
This open-source project
connects the Loggregator

00:01:05.762 --> 00:01:07.220
to Stackdriver
logging and metrics.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.490
It is available on GitHub as
part of our Stackdriver tools

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:11.810
BOSH release.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.560
Alpha, can you show us how to
get set up with the nozzle?

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.560
ALPHA CHEN: Let's do it.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.100
First, we need to enroll our
GCP project and the Stackdriver

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.930
metrics API, and
ensure our quotes are

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.077
set to the appropriate limits
as documented on our GitHub.

00:01:24.077 --> 00:01:26.660
Deploying the nozzle can be done
by modifying the BOSH release

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.957
or deploying the tile through
PCF Operations Manager.

00:01:28.957 --> 00:01:31.040
JEFF JOHNSON: Let's use
the BOSH release for this.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.360
ALPHA CHEN: Sounds good.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.180
To deploy the Stackdriver
nozzle, first upload the latest

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.150
release from our GitHub
repo to our BOSH director,

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:42.160
and then add the
release to the manifest.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.130
Now create a pool of nozzles
with some light configuration

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.590
to connect to Loggregator
in our BOSH manifest.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.381
JEFF JOHNSON: This
sounds like a great time

00:01:49.381 --> 00:01:52.260
for, and here's one
I prepared earlier.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.890
ALPHA CHEN: Let's
paste it in for now.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.181
We'll have a more detailed
example in our GitHub linked

00:01:56.181 --> 00:01:57.430
in the description below.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:00.077
Let's also run the world's
fastest BOSH deploy.

00:02:00.077 --> 00:02:00.910
JEFF JOHNSON: Voila!

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:02.530
So, we've got logging.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:03.360
Let's prove it.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.880
Logs generated by
our application

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.570
are streamed into the
Stackdriver logging console.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.310
You can see the logs appear in
near real-time as they happen.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.120
The logs we generated show
up with detailed metadata

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.020
related to the GC instance and
the Cloud Foundry application.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.210
One great use of this
is to filter down

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.910
to logs from a
specific application.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.340
Streaming logs can be
useful, but often, you

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.430
want to perform aggregations
and analysis on your data.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.730
This can be done in real-time
by creating a log sync

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.160
from Stackdriver into BigQuery.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.960
ALPHA CHEN: Every
message that CF generates

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.860
maintains a structure which
we can use in our analysis.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.510
Let's see what unique
user agents have

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.390
hit our Cloud Foundry today.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:52.181
JEFF JOHNSON: Sure.

00:02:52.181 --> 00:02:56.220
Let me test my minimal
BigQuery knowledge.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.310
So we want to filter by
HTTP start stop events.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.060
And I'm going to add a
simple GROUP BY statement.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.380
OK, just find it down here.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.060
Here's that structure
we talked about.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.970
And I'll just go ahead
and select that value,

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.730
so we can see it.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:18.480
And there you go.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.021
There's a list of
all the user agents

00:03:20.021 --> 00:03:21.420
that hit our foundry today.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:22.200
ALPHA CHEN: That's awesome!

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.783
So now we have a
full logging pipeline

00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:26.629
backed by CF, that Stackdriver,
and Google BigQuery.

00:03:26.629 --> 00:03:28.170
So let's take a look
at those metrics

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.409
we've been talking about.

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:31.200
Why don't we open up
Stackdriver monitoring

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.710
and take a look at our
user-created dashboard?

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.050
JEFF JOHNSON: This is
another cooking show moment,

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.940
but the creation
process was just

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.480
Add Chart, click, click, click.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.806
So we'll just go
ahead and skip it.

00:03:42.806 --> 00:03:44.430
ALPHA CHEN: CF exposes
a ton of metrics

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.917
on the health of your Cloud
Foundry and applications.

00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:48.750
The Stackdriver nozzle
handles these metrics

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.597
and sends them into
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:54.180
This dashboard goes beyond what
we can measure at the VM level

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.820
and gives us a look into
the huge number of metrics

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.689
that Cloud Foundry
exposes on each container.

00:03:59.689 --> 00:04:00.480
JEFF JOHNSON: Cool.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.020
So we can see information like
CPU activity of an application.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:08.790
And we can do that at the space,
org, or even application level.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.340
ALPHA CHEN: This sort of view
lets operators and developers

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.020
better understand
their applications

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.080
and make scaling decisions.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.202
The user can also create
alerts to detect abnormalities.

00:04:18.202 --> 00:04:20.160
JEFF JOHNSON: And that's
application monitoring

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.619
with Cloud Foundry
and Stackdriver.

00:04:21.619 --> 00:04:23.909
ALPHA CHEN: And thanks for
watching our series on Cloud

00:04:23.909 --> 00:04:24.952
Foundry and Stackdriver.

